WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:06.400
Between 2005 and 14, only 39% of top films
were actually original,

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.070
the other 61% were sequels, prequels, re-makes,
re-tellings, spin-offs, or adaptations.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:17.320
Hollywood loves taking what’s already popular
- a book, true story, or another movie, copying

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.630
and pasting, and then swimming in money.

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:23.570
The same stories, told over and over and over
again.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.519
And don’t get me wrong, A good movie is
a good movie, sequel or not.

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:28.519
But then there’s…

00:00:28.519 --> 00:00:29.519
Paul Blart 2.

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:31.080
Mixed reviews, let’s say.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:36.670
Of course studios like duplicating their success,
but first and foremost, studios like profit,

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:41.879
so why not make what people want: less unnecessary
sequels and more original stories?

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:48.901
Hollywood is so repetitive not because it’s
run out of ideas, or lacks creativity, but

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:51.200
because, as always: money.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.469
The problem with movies is that they’re
a huge upfront investment with a crazy amount

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:56.469
of risk.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:00:59.300
It’s a business of big winners and big losers.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.480
Like, 140 million dollar losers.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.299
Man, these are some uncanny valley faces.

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:08.520
In fact, every other movie fails to make a
profit.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.619
But those you never hear about, probably why
they failed.

00:01:11.619 --> 00:01:16.560
The ones you do hear about are the top 6%
making half of all the profit.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.799
The saying in the industry is nobody knows
anything - not a comforting thing to hear

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:23.980
when deciding where to put 100 million dollars.

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.210
Sometimes it looks more like gambling than
investing.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.000
There are just far safer places to put your
money.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.080
Like, anywhere else....BITCONNECT...Okay almost anywhere else.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:39.380
So, there might be demand for more original
stories, But with this much money, even a

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.310
little risk is a lot of risk.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:46.040
Steven Spielberg predicts it would only take
a few high-budget films to fail for the entire

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.090
industry to change forever.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.340
So studios have to optimize for every cent
they can.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.490
It’s been almost a decade since James Cameron
released Avatar.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:07.810
Or Titanic with blue people

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.690
And it’s still the most profitable movie
ever, by a good margin,

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.330
But let me ask you: how much do you actually
remember?

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:16.000
I’m willing to bet it doesn’t really hold
up.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.330
For all the excitement, and boy was there
a lot of it, it just doesn’t have anywhere

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:24.270
near the influence of a Star Wars, or Avengers,
or Harry Potter.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.200
So why was it so successful?

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.600
Obviously a lot of it was this and this.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:34.020
which, now less impressive, makes me a little
skeptical about the four sequels planned until

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.020
2025.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.940
Hey, you do you, James.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.410
But what Avatar got really right was wide
appeal.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.200
There are four big demographics in the film
industry:

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.480
Men and women under 25, and over 25.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.990
Making a really successful movie means targeting
all of them.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:55.590
Think Star Wars, Indiana Jones, Hunger Games,
Jurassic Park, everything from Pixar.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:01.890
These Four Quadrant Movies are the dream of
every studio - huge audiences, huge sequels,

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:02.970
lots of merchandise.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:07.950
But because so many movies try this same strategy,
they end up following the same pattern:

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.810
Lots of action and adventure, very obvious
good guys and bad guys, and a steady stream

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.110
of simple humor.

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:19.790
Ideally, at least one character each quadrant
can relate to, Young and old, relatively speaking

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:20.950
men and women.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:24.620
But one demographic is especially important:
Teenagers.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:29.200
AMC was so desperate to attract them, they
thought hey, kids can’t get off their dang

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.100
telephones, let’s just allow texting in
our theaters!

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.130
Which, luckily, didn’t go so well…

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:38.750
And because teenagers generally like seeing
a little more than talking rodents, studios

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:41.120
like to avoid G and PG movies.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.340
R is usually too limiting, which puts PG-13
right in the Goldilocks zone.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:49.520
These four characters can make or break a
film.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:54.320
41% of movies are rated PG-13, and they make
nearly half of all the profit.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.370
Which makes sense: the industry itself created
the rating after parents took their kids to

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:01.620
Temple of Doom or Gremlins and weren’t so
happy.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.220
Remember that microwave scene?

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:06.820
yikes Also, Kali Ma.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:11.370
And while YouTubers censor themselves to avoid
getting demonetized, Hollywood kinda does

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:12.370
the opposite.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:17.120
A nice, wholesome story in danger of getting
too soft a rating might sprinkle in some swearing

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.220
or violence, pushing themselves into golden
PG-13 territory.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.979
And there are may be rules about marketing
these to children, but movies like Avatar

00:04:24.979 --> 00:04:26.969
show they don’t really matter.

00:04:26.969 --> 00:04:30.870
Just sell your characters to toy companies,
who technically, aren’t advertising the

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.150
film itself.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.699
So who’s in charge of ratings?

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:38.650
Who determines the success of your years and
years of hard work and multi-million dollar

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:39.650
investment?

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:43.780
In the U.S., you have two options: Send your
movie to the Motion Picture Association of

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.900
America, where you don’t know who’s rating,
what their qualifications are, and don’t

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:48.900
have any recourse,

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.599
Or don’t get shown in 99% of theaters.

00:04:51.599 --> 00:04:52.599
Monopoly?

00:04:52.599 --> 00:04:53.960
What are you talking about?!…

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.370
Their mission is to “protect the creative
and artistic freedoms of storytellers”…

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.300
just not independent ones, or anyone making
what they don’t like

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.460
Someone was reading a bit too much Orwell when they wrote that.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:09.420
Oh, and don’t think the ratings actually
mean much, PG-13 movies actually have more

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.520
gun-violence than those rated R.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.439
And the shenanigans don’t stop here.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.180
The dream of any big company is to own everything.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:25.419
If you’re an oil company, you want to find
oil, drill for oil, transport the oil, refine

00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:27.569
the oil, And there will be blood

00:05:27.569 --> 00:05:32.180
Every company you involve shaves off a bit
of profit, until there isn’t a drop left

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:33.180
for you.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.960
Which is why movie studios used to own the
theaters,

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:40.120
By controlling the showings, they could make
sure their Paul Blart Six wouldn’t have

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:42.219
to compete with someone else’s Avatar.

00:05:42.219 --> 00:05:46.300
But then the U.S. Supreme Court said Get Out
of here, you need A Separation, and turned

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:47.930
the industry Inside Out.

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:53.229
What was one industry became two intertwined,
with the goal of weakening the studios.

00:05:53.229 --> 00:05:56.280
But 70 years later, they still have all the
power.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.150
Because they don’t need any one theater
to make a profit, but theaters desperately

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:01.319
need them.

00:06:01.319 --> 00:06:05.270
When you buy a ticket, your money is split
between them, depending on when you go.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:09.779
At the beginning, most goes to the studio,
which slowly changes over time.

00:06:09.779 --> 00:06:14.360
Because of this, theaters usually lose money
early on, then squeeze out a little profit

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:15.360
by the end.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:16.360
But that’s if they’re lucky.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:19.150
When it comes to Star Wars, the force is with
Disney.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:24.099
They can pretty much set the rules, theaters
can and do get angry, and then, of course,

00:06:24.099 --> 00:06:25.099
they agree anyway.

00:06:25.099 --> 00:06:28.860
Why does a literally 40 cent bag of popcorn
cost $4?

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:33.449
Because it’s the only money they don’t
have to share, sometimes their only profit.

00:06:33.449 --> 00:06:37.520
And studios make such a big deal about opening
weekends because that’s when they make most

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:38.520
of the money.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.720
But exactly how much is hard to say,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.419
When a studio starts making a movie, it forms
a new company, gives it the budget, and then

00:06:45.419 --> 00:06:47.009
pulls a clever bamboozle.

00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:51.930
It can say Hey, new company, you owe us some
fees, take most of its money, and make the

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.669
movie look unprofitable.

00:06:53.669 --> 00:06:57.729
Harry Potter and the Order of the Phoenix
made $450 million dollars,

00:06:57.729 --> 00:07:02.120
But when workers reached out their hands,
The studio said “So sorry, but there’s

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.819
just no money to give, the movie isn’t profitable”.

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:08.419
How does a Harry potter movie lose $167 million
dollars?

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:09.620
Clever Accounting.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:13.620
The studio took a total of $350 million in
fees,

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:18.400
Plus, a film’s budget is only a fraction
of its total cost, marketing alone can cost

00:07:18.409 --> 00:07:22.360
just as much, plus manufacturing, and overhead,
and so on.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:25.680
Most profit is made not domestically, but
internationally.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.500
And after theaters, studios squeeze as much
as possible from DVDs, Pay Per View, cable

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:32.970
TV, merchandise, video games, and music.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:37.499
The industry may not be sustainable, Companies
like MoviePass are trying to save it by offering

00:07:37.499 --> 00:07:39.620
unlimited movies for a $10 a month,

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:45.280
But let’s say you see a normally $10 movie
every day for a month, minus the $10 subscription,

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.210
and they’ve lost $290 just on you, just
in one month.

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:53.930
If the business fails, we’ll all be conditioned
to unlimited movies, and may not like going

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:55.479
back to the old prices.

00:07:55.479 --> 00:07:59.779
But as sites like Netflix and Amazon rise
from the ashes, things will probably change

00:07:59.779 --> 00:08:00.779
for the better.

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:05.059
anyone can watch their favorite movies from
home, or actually make them themselves,

00:08:05.059 --> 00:08:08.479
Seriously If your dream is to make films,
or Youtube videos, That’s something you

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:09.759
can actually just start learning.

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:14.089
and SkillShare is the place for this, with
video courses taught by the experts.

00:08:14.089 --> 00:08:17.400
You can learn script writing from a New York
Times Bestselling Author, so you can make

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.539
the most interesting videos possible.

00:08:19.539 --> 00:08:23.819
You can start drawing a beautiful movie poster
or illustrations for your video from a course

00:08:23.819 --> 00:08:24.819
like this one.

00:08:24.819 --> 00:08:27.680
When you’re ready to start filming, you
can practice shooting and editing.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.100
This one teaches you how you can get great
